00:33:01.850 --> 00:33:11.400
Bump goes away. When Bump went to find Birdie one morning, she flew straight over his head.

00:33:12.280 --> 00:33:18.570
I can't play now, she whistled. I've got to do some singing.

00:33:21.210 --> 00:33:24.310
Out of the way, Bump, squeak whizzer. I'm in a hurry.

00:33:26.260 --> 00:33:29.220
As for McDove, he barked at Bump for treading on his rubber bone.

00:33:30.510 --> 00:33:34.190
which made him bump into a tree where Jay was sitting,

00:33:34.410 --> 00:33:38.030
who squawked so crossly it made Bump step backwards.

00:33:38.350 --> 00:33:39.770
Mind me, said Munch.

00:33:40.840 --> 00:33:43.860
Ouch, cried Bump, as he sat down very hard,

00:33:45.150 --> 00:33:47.630
but Bump had no time to feel sorry for himself.

00:33:48.470 --> 00:33:49.970
Up popped Big Bun.

00:33:51.090 --> 00:33:54.110
You do know you're sitting on our newest and nicest hole, he said.

00:33:55.560 --> 00:33:59.970
Nobody loves me, sniffed Bump, a tear rolling down his...

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:08.480
cheek. I'm just a big nuisance to everyone. Bump decided to go away, but not before he had said

00:34:08.480 --> 00:34:14.990
goodbye to all his friends. Goodbye, Whizzer. I won't be getting in your way anymore. Goodbye,

00:34:15.150 --> 00:34:20.160
Macduff. I'm sorry I trod on your bone. And you won't have to hide in your shell because of me,

00:34:20.380 --> 00:34:29.020
Bump told Munch. Goodbye, little bun. Goodbye, big bun, he called, marching on.

00:34:30.730 --> 00:34:33.850
Bump's last goodbye was for his special friend, Birdie.

00:34:34.930 --> 00:34:36.410
Thank you for being so kind to me.

00:34:38.470 --> 00:34:40.090
But where are you going? she asked.

00:34:41.540 --> 00:34:44.840
Somewhere where I don't make people cross all the time, Bump said.

00:34:48.460 --> 00:34:51.500
I'm sure nobody meant to be cross, whistled Birdie.

00:34:54.720 --> 00:34:56.850
But Bump did not want to listen.

00:35:01.120 --> 00:35:05.060
When his friends realized they'd never see him again, they were all very sad.

00:35:06.180 --> 00:35:08.580
We didn't mean to be nasty to Bump, squeaked Whizzer.

00:35:09.660 --> 00:35:12.150
But how can we get him to come back, growled Macduff.

00:35:13.900 --> 00:35:16.440
We have to find a way to cheer him up, said Birdie.

00:35:18.350 --> 00:35:18.870
I know.

00:35:22.030 --> 00:35:24.790
With this, she whistled to her brothers and sisters.

00:35:30.510 --> 00:35:34.540
As he walked along all alone, Bump felt even more miserable.

00:35:36.050 --> 00:35:37.950
He did not really want to leave his friends.

00:35:39.070 --> 00:35:41.150
Just thinking about them made him cry.

00:35:42.120 --> 00:35:45.340
Then, suddenly, he heard a faint sound.

00:35:46.690 --> 00:35:51.080
A pretty sound made him feel much better.

00:35:53.590 --> 00:35:57.660
Birdie and her family, they're singing my favorite song.

00:36:09.310 --> 00:36:20.650
Bump couldn't help himself. It was such a pretty song. He just had to dance. Before he knew it, Bump had danced right back to his other friends.

00:36:22.050 --> 00:36:29.900
We're sorry we upset you, Bump, said Little Bun. We all love you very much, said Birdie, giving Bump a big kiss.

00:36:30.800 --> 00:36:33.340
I love you all too, said Bump.

00:37:01.080 --> 00:37:15.760
One day, Mr. Gruber decided to take Paddington on a mystery outing.

00:37:16.520 --> 00:37:18.200
He gave him a bar of chocolate for his lunch,

00:37:18.220 --> 00:37:21.070
and they set out early in order to avoid the crowds.

00:37:22.330 --> 00:37:23.910
Paddington liked outings with Mr. Gruber,

00:37:23.990 --> 00:37:27.190
and to have a mystery thrown in as well seemed very good value indeed.

00:37:28.640 --> 00:37:29.680
Wait until you get inside.

00:37:30.030 --> 00:37:32.130
said Mr. Gruber. You're in for a surprise.

00:37:33.460 --> 00:37:35.660
Paddington grew more and more mystified.

00:37:36.200 --> 00:37:39.080
The building seemed to be full of people in strange clothes,

00:37:39.260 --> 00:37:40.620
standing about doing nothing.

00:37:41.710 --> 00:37:43.670
I shan't be a moment, Mr. Gruber, he called.

00:37:44.020 --> 00:37:45.560
I just want to buy some souvenirs.

00:37:47.300 --> 00:37:49.160
He hurried over to a nearby kiosk

00:37:49.280 --> 00:37:52.490
and raised his hat politely several times

00:37:52.490 --> 00:37:54.580
to the lady behind the counter.

00:37:56.160 --> 00:37:58.920
But for some strange reason, she didn't reply.

00:38:00.380 --> 00:38:02.000
Excuse me, he announced.

00:38:02.240 --> 00:38:04.120
I'd like three large postcards, please.

00:38:04.380 --> 00:38:07.120
One for my scrapbook, one for Aunt Lucy, and one for Mr. Gruber.

00:38:09.240 --> 00:38:11.360
I don't think you're going to be very lucky.

00:38:11.800 --> 00:38:13.860
I think she's made of wax, Mr. Brown.

00:38:14.350 --> 00:38:16.270
Made of wax, exclaimed Paddington.

00:38:16.760 --> 00:38:18.300
He gave the lady a hard stare.

00:38:19.140 --> 00:38:21.000
I've never heard of anyone being made of wax.

00:38:21.910 --> 00:38:24.830
You'll find a lot of people like that in here, Mr. Brown.

00:38:25.170 --> 00:38:26.990
Most of them aren't real at all.

00:38:27.310 --> 00:38:29.790
It's what's known as a waxworks museum.

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:37.080
Mr. Gruber went off to buy some tickets, and after he'd gone, Paddington gazed round the hall.

00:38:38.220 --> 00:38:41.240
Apart from having no souvenirs, his chocolate was beginning to melt.

00:38:42.010 --> 00:38:45.270
Not wishing to offend his friend, he looked for somewhere to deposit it,

00:38:46.720 --> 00:38:49.440
and nearby he thought he saw the very thing.

00:38:50.080 --> 00:38:51.490
Here, said the attendant.

00:38:51.920 --> 00:38:54.300
What do you think you're doing? That's my ticket box.

00:38:54.500 --> 00:38:57.900
It's not for the likes of young bears to drop their remains in.

00:38:58.720 --> 00:38:59.980
I'm very sorry, excuse me.

00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:05.640
Paddington. I thought you were a waxworks like everyone else. Waxworks? I'll give you waxworks.

00:39:05.740 --> 00:39:14.040
Come back here. But Paddington was already making good his escape. Hurrying down some stairs,

00:39:14.220 --> 00:39:18.860
as fast as his legs would carry him, he found himself in another large room full of figures.

00:39:20.090 --> 00:39:22.930
Worse still, he could hear the sound of voices getting nearer and nearer.

00:39:23.460 --> 00:39:27.780
He decided there was only one thing for it. He would have to become a waxworks himself.

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:36.710
group, said a voice, represents England at the time of Charles I. In the middle is Oliver Cromwell,

00:39:36.970 --> 00:39:42.850
